From 1a1c49c59dc9f3e86bb16587b00c680170b5165a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ajensenwaud <ajensenwaud@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 04:15:58 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 14/26] ling: one attention-partials area shared by every
 Buffers (prompt, verify window, shared verify, MTP rows), sized once for the
 widest; saves 3 x 1.88 GiB a rank at 1M on the full pack at TP=2, results
 unchanged

Each fw.Buffers held its own MLA attention partials (po/pm/pl, nch x ar x heads x (2048 + 8) B, capped at att_bytes
2 GiB). The engine had four: bp (prompt chunks), bd (one stream's verify window, also keep's commit), bs (shared
verify) and the MTP head's bm. They now point at one state.Att, allocated once by Engine.init for the largest rows
any of them takes at the capacity (att.ar = max attRows; nch is the same for all). Each Buffers keeps its own nch
and ar, so every attention call is cut into the same row blocks and launched with the same arguments as before;
only the partials' base address moves (256-byte aligned as before). A standalone Buffers (checks) passes null and
keeps its own partials.

Why no two of them can hold live partials at once (code reading):
- The partials carry nothing between calls. ops.attention launches tf_ling_chunks_r16 then tf_glm_merge on the same
  stream. The chunk kernel writes po/pm/pl for every (chunk c < nch, row r < rows, head h < heads) it is launched
  over, live chunk or not (no early return: an empty chunk stores m = -inf, l = 0), and the merge reads exactly
  that set into b.ol. block_mla.zig is the only user of b.partials.
- Every launch of every Buffers goes on the engine's one in-order stream (Engine.stream): prefill/chunkRun (bp,
  and the MTP absorb on bp after the main stack), verify and keep (bd), verifyShared (bs: concurrent lanes' windows
  become ONE forward, segments in row order, attention per segment one after another), draft (bm: absorb and the
  chained drafts, one after another). The MTP head's Graph is built on the same stream. No other stream or thread
  launches forwards: the lanes backend calls the Engine serially (it has one bound sequence, e.cur), and TP rank 1
  replays rank 0's steps through its own Engine on its own single stream. Weight loading uses the stream only
  before the Buffers exist. So call N's merge completes before call N+1's chunk kernel starts, whichever Buffers
  either belongs to. Ling has no CUDA graph capture (native.zig: no captured set), so no replay can reorder them.
- Verify windows: verify then keep on bd, the next verify or draft after; a draft's bm forwards come after the
  verify that produced its hidden rows (rows_at / hold point into bd/bs/bp's normed rows, not the partials).
Hence all four can share; none had to stay separate.

Checks (TP=1, against the previous build e69d70d3): the Ling regression chain (16
commands) plus ling-lanes --mtp (bf16, fp8) and ling-init-faults --mtp --every 3, all EXIT 0 on both builds;
served ids and routing picks (ling-lanes, plain and --mtp, bf16 and fp8) and ling-exl3-layers' layer outputs
byte-equal; every RESULT/PASS line identical except the fault harness's call counts (+2 driver calls, +1 failure
point: the new allocation and its zero fill).
